# DURABLE REPRO — assigning to a `String` element inside a `for … in &coll`
# loop DOUBLE-FREES at runtime.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg, C backend): `gg check` reports "OK: no
# semantic errors", `gg build` succeeds, and the binary aborts with
#
#   free(): double free detected in tcache 2
#
# ⚠ THIS IS THE MEMORY-UNSAFE CELL OF A TYPE AXIS THAT IS OTHERWISE MERELY
# WRONG OR CORRECT. Measured across element types, same loop shape, all four
# `gg check`-clean:
#
#   element type   mutation                 outcome
#   int            e = e + 100              write LOST (prints 1, want 101)
#   struct         e.v = e.v + 100          WRITES THROUGH correctly (101)
#   Vector[int]    e.push(9)                WRITES THROUGH correctly (len 2)
#   String         e = e + "!"              **DOUBLE FREE** (this fixture)
#
# So "element write-through through a loop iterable is lost" — which is what
# the reference said before this fixture landed — is wrong twice over: it is
# false for struct and Vector elements, and for String it understates a
# memory-safety defect as a lost write. That is a Core #8 event (a known defect
# documented as benign) on top of a Core #12 one (a whole typed axis
# generalised from one cell).
#
# PROVENANCE — BISECTED 2026-07-26. A REGRESSION ~6.5 weeks old, introduced by
# a LEAK FIX: first bad commit `a367f2c3` (2026-06-11), "register print/format
# String temps for drop at their birth — the print-temp leak class". Parent
# `43a58519` prints `x` (write LOST but memory-SAFE); `a367f2c3` double-frees.
# So the shape was never correct — the write has always been dropped — but it
# degraded from wrong-output to memory-unsafe there. A fix must not simply
# revert that commit or the print-temp leak class returns.
#
# LIKELY MECHANISM (not chased to the writer site — do not treat as
# established): the loop element binding is a borrow into the collection's
# storage; assigning a freshly-built `String` into it appears to store the new
# buffer without releasing ownership of the old one from the element slot, so
# both the element slot and the temporary end up owning the same allocation.
# The struct/Vector cells work because their mutations are in-place through the
# borrow rather than a whole-slot assignment.
#
# INTENDED: the loop writes through — the program prints `x!` — and runs clean
# under ASan. If the language instead decides a whole-element assignment under
# `&`-iteration is not expressible, the reference-grade answer is a CHECK-TIME
# rejection; an accepted program that double-frees is wrong under either
# reading (Core #10, lower-or-reject).
#
# ⚠ RELATED BUT DISTINCT, already filed: (a) the `int`-element write loss
# (D33's second open item — the Family-1 projected-TYPE bug); (b) the
# COMPREHENSION form's int-to-pointer conversion warning. Neither is this:
# those are lost writes, this is heap corruption.
#
# ORACLE (ggdef): a value-semantics interpreter cannot observe this class at
# all — Core #13 says ASan on the real backends is the adjudicating instrument
# here, not ggdef.
#
# The seed is COMPUTED and the strings are heap-forced (built by
# concatenation, not literals) so no const-fold or static-storage path can
# elide the allocation — a literal here would be a false negative.
#
# Cited from TODO.md (CoW / ownership / High).

String seed():
    return "x"

void main():
    String s0 = seed()
    String s1 = seed() + "y"
    Vector[String] a = [s0, s1]
    for e in &a:
        e = e + "!"
    print(a.get(0).unwrap())
